---
// for-cooperatives.astro
//
// PRIMARY JOB (PUBLIC_SITE_IA §3): what ICN would mean for an institution like
// yours, and how you could realistically approach it. Explicitly NOT its job:
// general explanation — link back rather than repeat.
//
// This page previously opened with three sections re-arguing the institutional
// diagnosis that /why-icn owns (one of them sharing a verbatim sentence with
// it) and the bridge-institutions framing that /cooperative-economy owns. Those
// are now a handoff. What replaced them is the question the page was actually
// missing: the adoption path.

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ScopeModel from '../components/ScopeModel.astro';
import ProvenanceTrail from '../components/ProvenanceTrail.astro';
import AdoptionPath from '../components/AdoptionPath.astro';
import MaturityBadge from '../components/MaturityBadge.astro';
import ReadingLadder from '../components/ReadingLadder.astro';
import { PROJECT_STATE as projectState, subsystemsInBand } from '../lib/projectState';

// Derived, never asserted. A hand-typed count here would be a second project
// tracker, which is exactly what /roadmap was before it was folded away.
const strongCount = subsystemsInBand('strong').length;
---

<Layout title="For Cooperatives" activeNav="for-cooperatives" description="What ICN is trying to do for cooperatives, communities, and federations — and what it is not yet ready to do.">

  <section class="section section-page-top">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="page-hero">
        <span class="badge badge-amber">For Cooperatives</span>
        <h1>ICN is institutional infrastructure for cooperatives and cooperative networks.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          For cooperatives, the point is not software novelty. It is to reduce dependence on generic tools
          and give governance, obligations, records, and federation a more reliable technical foundation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-grid truth-grid-2">
        <div class="truth-note truth-note-teal">
          <span class="truth-note-label">Partner value</span>
          <h2 class="intro-card-title">Continuity, accountability, and interoperability</h2>
          <p>
            The value is not only better tooling. It is stronger institutional memory,
            more credible governance records, and better coordination across cooperative relationships.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="truth-note truth-note-amber">
          <span class="truth-note-label">Strategic direction</span>
          <h2 class="intro-card-title">Cooperative infrastructure, co-operated and collectively held</h2>
          <p>
            The larger goal is infrastructure that the movement uses, governs, and improves together —
            owned by the institutions that depend on it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt section-pre-ladder">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <!-- ─── Diagnosis handoff ────────────────────────────────────────────
           Three sections used to live here — "Bridges, and what the bridge
           does not cover", "The institutional problem you actually face", and
           "Why generic software is not enough". All three re-argued a case
           /why-icn already makes better, one of them with a verbatim-identical
           sentence ("Integrating them only spreads the gap more evenly"), and
           the bridge-institutions argument is /cooperative-economy's whole
           premise. PUBLIC_SITE_IA §3 names this page's duplication as
           outstanding and its contract as "Adoption and evaluation for an
           institution — general explanation is NOT its job; link back rather
           than repeat." This is the link back. -->
      <h2>You already run all of this somewhere</h2>
      <p>
        Membership in a CRM or a spreadsheet. Minutes in a shared drive, separate
        from the decisions they record. Votes in one tool, the result pasted into
        another. Books that have no idea what was decided. Work that happens
        because a particular person remembered.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are not going to re-argue that here — <a href="/why-icn">Why ICN</a>
        makes the case that this is structural rather than a tooling problem, and
        <a href="/cooperative-economy">The cooperative economy</a> covers where
        credit unions, CDFIs, and fiscal sponsors fit. This page assumes you are
        past that argument and asks the two questions that follow it:
        <strong>what would this actually mean for an institution like ours</strong>,
        and <strong>how would we realistically get there from where we are?</strong>
      </p>

      <h2>What changes when the institutional loop is closed</h2>
      <p>
        ICN is built on the claim that the pieces of institutional life should not be scattered
        across unrelated systems. When the loop closes, a cooperative gets things that are genuinely hard to get today:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A member's <strong>standing</strong> is provable — not a row in a CRM, but a cryptographic fact the institution can verify directly.</li>
        <li>A decision made in a meeting has a <strong>durable, pointable existence</strong> — not as minutes someone wrote up afterward, but as an artifact of the institution itself, tied to the members who held standing in it.</li>
        <li>A rule the membership agreed to is <strong>visible at the point where it matters</strong>, not buried in a document that gets read once a year.</li>
        <li>Obligations between members or between cooperatives are carried as <strong>institutional facts</strong> — with treasury, budgets and approvals, patronage settlement, and mutual-credit positions — not entries in a private spreadsheet.</li>
        <li>The chain from <em>what we decided</em> to <em>what actually happened</em> stays intact, so the institution can answer its own members honestly about its own actions.</li>
        <li>Federation with other cooperatives is a first-class mechanism — <strong>formal agreements, attestations, and cross-institutional clearing</strong> — not something two organizations have to hold together by email and trust in specific staff.</li>
        <!-- A seventh bullet claimed shared compute/storage "can be held as a
             commons with governance, placement, clearing, and dispute
             resolution". docs/status.toml has compute at foundation-only and
             storage at simplified. In an un-banded list of things a cooperative
             "gets", that reads as available capability. Cut rather than
             caveated — the list is about evaluation, and a capability this far
             from usable does not belong in an evaluation checklist. -->
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this replaces the work of running a cooperative.
        It changes what the work is resting on.
      </p>

      <ScopeModel title="The contexts your members would actually act inside" showTruthLabelChip />

      <h2>What you would be adopting today</h2>
      <p>
        The strongest thing ICN has is the chain below: an outcome that can be
        walked back through the rule that shaped it, the decision that authorized
        it, and the standing that made acting legitimate.
      </p>

      <div class="trail-wrap">
        <ProvenanceTrail title="The chain a decision walks" showTruthLabelChip />
      </div>

      <!-- This used to be four paragraphs of hand-written per-subsystem maturity
           prose. /whats-real-now generates the same account from docs/status.toml
           and states outright that where the two disagree it wins and the other
           page is a defect. A second hand-maintained copy is how /roadmap drifted
           a month behind canonical state before it was folded in. The summary
           below is derived; the judgement after it is this page's own, and is
           about evaluation rather than about subsystems. -->
      <p>
        Of {projectState.subsystems.length} subsystems tracked against source,
        {strongCount} are in the <MaturityBadge band="strong" /> band
        (verified {projectState.newestVerified}). That is a real foundation, and
        it is also not the thing you would be living in.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The gap you would feel first is the member-facing surface.</strong>
        The capabilities underneath are genuinely built; the unified interface an
        ordinary member would open is the part of the system furthest from what it
        needs to be. An institution adopting ICN this month would be adopting
        strong substrate and an unfinished front door — and would need people
        willing to work in that gap.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full per-subsystem account, with the project's own record of what is
        missing in each, is at <a href="/whats-real-now">What's real now</a>. If
        anything on this page reads as more finished than it does there, that page
        is right and this one is a defect worth reporting.
      </p>

      <h2 id="adoption">How you would actually get there</h2>
      <p>
        The most common and most reasonable objection to everything above is that
        you already depend on all of it. Membership is in the CRM, the books are in
        QuickBooks, and the cooperative cannot stop operating for a quarter while
        someone rebuilds its nervous system.
      </p>
      <p>
        It should not have to. The design intent is that ICN can be adopted one
        bounded function at a time, with the incumbent system still running
        underneath, and with every stage reversible.
      </p>

      <div class="trail-wrap">
        <AdoptionPath showTruthLabelChip />
      </div>

      <p>
        Two things worth being explicit about. First, this is a
        <strong>sequence, not a product</strong> — there is no migration tool, and
        the honest reading of the figure above is that the middle of the path is
        the least built part of ICN. Second, the unit of adoption is
        <strong>one function</strong>, not one organization. A cooperative that
        moves only its safety-spending decisions onto ICN and leaves everything
        else where it is has adopted ICN correctly.
      </p>

      <!-- The prose that opened this section restated the "co-operated,
           collectively held" framing already made by the second card at the top
           of this page. What was load-bearing were the two caveats, so only
           those remain. -->
      <h2 id="how-cooperative-infrastructure-works">Two things to be clear about</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You would be running it, or sharing that with peers.</strong> ICN is not hosted for you. There is no vendor on the other end of it, which is the point and also the cost.</li>
        <li><strong>ICN sits underneath your governance, not in place of it.</strong> Deliberation, interpretation, and political judgment stay with the members. The substrate carries what they decided, not the deciding.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- One section, not two. "How to engage" and "How to engage right now"
           were adjacent headings answering the same question — the first in
           abstract prose, the second as steps. The steps survived; the prose
           became the one-line framing on each. -->
      <h2>What to actually do next</h2>
      <p>
        Most cooperatives should be observing, not adopting. That is a real
        relationship and the honest one for the current state of the system.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Evaluate first.</strong> Read <a href="/whats-real-now">What's real now</a>
          and decide whether the current maturity is compatible with what your
          institution actually needs. This is the step most cooperatives should
          stop at for now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Name the function you would move first.</strong> Stage 01 of the
          path above needs no involvement from us. Working out where your
          membership, rules, decisions, money, and work really live — and who can
          change each one — is useful to a cooperative whether or not it ever
          adopts ICN.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bring a concrete institutional case.</strong> Open a
          <a href="https://github.com/InterCooperative-Network/icn/discussions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Discussion</a>
          describing the governance, accounting, coordination, or federation
          problem your current stack does not carry well. Institutional knowledge
          of how cooperatives actually run is the input the project is shortest on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>If you only want to support the work,</strong> the GitHub Sponsors
          rail is on <a href="/get-involved#support">Get involved</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <!-- A closing section titled "This is about enhancing human institutions,
           not automating them away" stood here. It made the same point as the
           second caveat above, which is two paragraphs earlier on this page, and
           /how-it-works owns the long version of the argument. Saying it three
           times does not make it three times truer. -->
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-ladder">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <ReadingLadder current="for-cooperatives">
        <Fragment slot="extras">
          <!-- "Community" pointed at /community, a redirect to /get-involved
               that sat next to a /get-involved button. -->
          <a href="/get-involved" class="btn btn-ghost">Get involved</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/InterCooperative-Network/icn" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ghost">GitHub ↗</a>
        </Fragment>
      </ReadingLadder>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Promoted from h3 to h2 so the outline does not skip a level after the
     page h1. Visual weight is unchanged — level and appearance are separate
     concerns, and the outline is the one screen readers navigate by. */
  .intro-card-title {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
  }

.section-page-top { padding-top: calc(64px + var(--space-3xl)); }
.narrow { max-width: 760px; }
.page-hero { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-md); align-items: flex-start; }
.page-hero .badge { align-self: flex-start; }
.page-hero h1 { margin: 0; font-size: clamp(1.625rem, 3.6vw, 2.25rem); line-height: 1.25; }
.lead { font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--text-body); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0; }
.prose h2 { margin-top: var(--space-2xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
.prose p { margin: 0 0 var(--space-md); line-height: 1.7; }
.prose ul { margin: var(--space-md) 0; padding-left: var(--space-lg); }
.prose li { margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); line-height: 1.7; }

/* Spacing wrapper around the ProvenanceTrail placement. The trail
   component itself uses margin: 0; this wrapper preserves the
   prose-rhythm breathing room that the previous .page-visual figure
   provided, so the paragraph that follows the trail does not sit
   flush against the dashed proof-flow footer. */
.trail-wrap { margin: var(--space-md) 0 var(--space-lg); }
</style>
